      "text": "For Ionic compounds, use the reactivity series as the organising idea. Physical properties describe how a substance behaves without changing its composition, whereas chemical properties describe reactions that produce new substances. In reaction questions, compare the positions of metals in the reactivity series before predicting displacement. Highly reactive metals are not usually obtained by simple carbon reduction; electrolysis is needed for the most reactive group. Moderately reactive metals may be extracted through roasting or calcination followed by reduction. In ionic compound questions, explain electron transfer and electrostatic attraction, then connect that structure to high melting point and electrical conduction in molten or aqueous form. For corrosion, explain exposure to environmental agents and then name a prevention method with its mechanism. Galvanisation is especially important because zinc provides a protective coating and can offer sacrificial protection. Amphoteric oxides such as Al₂O₃ and ZnO are common board exceptions and should be memorised with both acid and base reactions. This expanded note is intended for board preparation: learn the principle, write the representative equation, identify the observation or product, and practise applying the same idea to a changed situation. Accuracy matters more than decorative wording. Chemical formulae, subscripts, coefficients, conditions and terminology should be checked before the final answer is written."
